import { len, print, to_str, union } from builtins;

// Reassigning a `mut` set from inside a nested scope, where the new value is
// built from an element borrowed out of an array.
//
// This is a regression test for a use-after-free: a `mut` set binding used to
// keep the *value-track* ownership model, where the sole reference on the
// binding's current value belongs to the scope that created that value rather
// than to the binding's own slot. That is fine while every assignment happens in
// the binding's own scope, and wrong the moment one happens in a nested one: the
// match arm's teardown released the value `acc` had just been assigned, so
// `acc.len()` after the match read a freed block (97 under `aipl run`, 2043
// under the case harness — a value that depends on how the process was started
// is freed memory, not an answer). The replaced value leaked in the same step,
// for the mirror-image reason: nothing owned it to release.
//
// The fix was to give sets and dicts the same slot-owned-reference model arrays
// and boxed types already had — see `mut_binding_owns_slot_ref` in
// `aipl-codegen`. The slot retains the incoming value and releases the outgoing
// one, so the arm's teardown only releases the arm's own reference.
//
// `union` is incidental — any heap value assigned from a nested scope did it,
// and the same shape with an array binding was already correct. It is kept here
// because the array element is what makes the shape realistic: this is the
// accumulate-over-a-list-of-sets loop, written bounded so a failure prints
// rather than aborts.
//
// Introduced by `25f946d` (sets and dicts as array elements).
fn main() !prints {
    let ls: #{i64}[] = [#{1}, #{2}];
    mut acc: #{i64} = #{9};
    match (ls[0]) {
        some(s) => {
            set acc = acc.union(s);
            print(acc.len().to_str());
        },
        none => print("unreachable"),
    };
    // The assignment outlives the arm that made it.
    print(acc.len().to_str());

    print(ls.len().to_str());

    // The loop this shape stands in for. Under the value-track model the
    // per-iteration teardown freed the accumulator every trip, and this form
    // aborted the process outright rather than printing a wrong number.
    //
    // `union_loop_union_all` flags it, correctly — `ls.union_all()` is the
    // spelling. The loop is the point here: the builtin is one call, and this
    // case exists to drive the per-iteration reassignment the bug lived in.
    mut all: #{i64} = #{}; #[allow]
    for (let s : ls) {
        set all = all.union(s);
    }
    print(all.len().to_str());
}
